  "2022 Q3": {"marks":5,"subject":"A bar chart's data, a claim, a test for two proportions, and the design of a study.","rows":[{"for":"state type of data","shows":"discrete numerical","part":"(a)"},{"for":"valid explanation","shows":"eg a claim cannot be made from a graph OR one clinic in Edinburgh is not representative of the UK","part":"(b)"},{"for":"state appropriate test","shows":"\\(z\\)-test for two proportions","part":"(c)"},{"for":"state an appropriate design condition","shows":"eg samples need to be chosen randomly","part":"(d)"},{"for":"valid explanation","shows":"eg they are not comparable as the data is not collected over the same time periods","part":"(e)"}],"notes":["(a) 1. Accept 'normally distributed', 'normal' or 'bell-shaped' at •¹. [sic]","(b) 1. Study only completed over a one-year period (not relevant to location), award 0/1.","(c) 1. A chi-squared test is also appropriate for •³.","(d) 1. Sample size must be the same (not appropriate for proportions) award 0/1.","(e) 1. There must be comparison between months or seasons or time periods or temperature between the two clinics.","(e) 2. •⁵ the seasonable variability could affect the results. [sic]","(e) 3. The time periods/seasons must be the same (same months), award 1/1."]},
